Comparative Architectural Analysis: MRR vs RMS Workflows
1. Architecture Overview
Aspect	MRR (material_workflow)	RMS (rms_workflow)
Graph nodes	20 registered nodes	6 registered nodes
Conditional edges	6 router functions	1 router function
Excel handling	Dedicated node + dedicated router	Embedded inside each agent node
Form handling	Dedicated node + dedicated router	Embedded inside creator agent
State machine	Multi-step (current_step: HEADER → ITEMS → EVALUATING)	Flat (single-pass per agent)
Checkpointer	MemorySaver for confirm/cancel flow	None
Entry point	Conditional (RBAC or Form parser)	Linear (parse_intent → permission_check)
2. Input Handling: MRR (Reference Pattern)
The MRR workflow has a properly modeled input handling system:

Graph structure:


session_entry_router → [conditional] → parse_intent_node OR parse_user_input
                                         ↓
                                  permission_check_node
                                         ↓
                                 rbac_intent_router → [6 paths]
                                         ↓ (creator path)
                                creator_router_entry
                                         ↓
                          creator_path_router → [6 destinations]
                            ├── "excel_handler"      → excel_handler_node → END
                            ├── "form_submit_handler" → form_submit_handler_node → pending_mrr_data
                            ├── "submit_mrr"          → mrr_submission_node → ai_verifier → auto_verifier → END
                            ├── "cancel_mrr"          → mrr_cancel_node → END
                            ├── "form_flow"           → parse_user_input → (step machine)
                            └── "creator_needs_file"  → creator_needs_file_node → END
creator_path_router (nodes.py:1496-1571) explicitly checks:

Form submissions (disambiguation, confirmation, cancellation)
Natural language confirm/cancel
Excel file uploads → routes to excel_handler
Fallback → creator_needs_file
This means the graph topology accurately reflects the business logic. Every code path has a corresponding node + edge.

